Large language models exhibit biases toward familiar statements and patterns from their training data. While biases may be useful heuristics for producing plausible text in some settings, they can undermine the models' ability to reason in an objective, logical manner.

_Contexutal calibration_ is a method for reducing the influence of these biases in settings like multiple choice tests, where models give short answers without reasoning. We adapt the idea of contextual calibration to multi-step reasoning traces. Our new method called _context relevance_ quantifies how much likelihood a model attributes to a reasoning step because of its specific context as opposed to the model's internal bias.

We evaluate context relevance as an indicator for reasoning correctness in three different datasets (MR-GSM8K, BIG-Bench Mistake, and ProcessBench) containing annotated solutions to mathematical and logical question. We compute the per-step context relevance for all 8,584 reasoning traces from the datasets using five different language models from the Qwen and Llama families. Across all models and datasets, steps with low context relevance consistently show a higher propensity for reasoning errors (AUROC 0.645-0.751). In one task category, AUROC is as high as 0.900.

As a baseline, we evaluate the models' ability to find reasoning errors when explicitly prompted to do so (_LLM judge_). Qwen3.5-27B, the strongest model we evaluated, acheives top error detection performance, outperforming models like GPT-4 that were some of the most capable LLMs available when the benchmarks were first published.

While the association between low context relevance and reasoning errors is robust, we find LLM judges are more accurate at detecting errors than the context relevance signal alone. While as a LLM judge, Qwen3.5-27B acheives x-y% accuracy for labeling exact error positions across the three benchmarks, a classifier based on thresholded context relevance only acheives x-y%.
